import LatticeProb.Bonanno.MatedCRTHarmonic.Frozen.IsCorrelatedBM2
import LatticeProb.Bonanno.MatedCRTHarmonic.RouteM.PsiBundle
import LatticeProb.Bonanno.LQGMetric.Complex.JordanMapCurve

/-!
# The Jordan frontier of the windows of the genuine space-filling SLE

The named hypothesis `WindowFrontierJordanGenuineCurve`: for fixed times `a < u < b` and almost every correlated
Brownian pair, one continuous proper surjection `g : ℝ → ℂ` whose fibres are the classes of the equivalence
generated by the direct identifications of the pair has `g(u)` in the interior of `g([a, b])` and a Jordan frontier
for the component of that interior containing `g(u)`. The whole-plane space-filling SLE on the quantum cone,
parametrized by quantum area, is such a curve. `WindowFrontierJordanTransfer.lean` derives
`WindowFrontierJordanInput 𝒮` from it.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false

open MeasureTheory Set Filter Topology
open MatedCRTHarmonic.MatedCRT (Path2 IsCorrelatedBM2)
open MatedCRTHarmonic.RouteM (directRel)

namespace MatedCRT

/-- **Named hypothesis: the windows of the space-filling SLE traversal of a correlated Brownian pair have Jordan
frontiers.** Truth-checked: TRUE.

For `0 < γ < 2`, the law `μ` of the `γ`-correlated two-sided Brownian pair (`IsCorrelatedBM2 γ μ`) and fixed times
`a < u < b`, for `μ`-almost every pair `z = (L, R)` there is a curve `g : ℝ → ℂ` such that
* `g` is continuous, onto `ℂ` and proper (`g t → ∞` as `|t| → ∞`);
* times identified directly by `L` or by `R` are merged by `g`, and `g s = g t` only for times equivalent under the
  equivalence generated by these direct identifications;
* `g(u)` lies in the interior of `g([a, b])`, and the frontier of the connected component of that interior containing
  `g(u)` is a Jordan curve.

The curve is the whole-plane space-filling SLE_κ', `κ' = 16/γ²`, on the `γ`-quantum cone, parametrized by `γ`-LQG
area with `g(0) = 0`, whose boundary-length pair, divided by its standard deviation at time one, is `z`
(Duplantier–Miller–Sheffield, arXiv:1409.7055, Theorem 1.9 and (1.8), p. 27: a correlated two-sided Brownian motion
for every `κ' > 4`, with correlation `-cos(4π/κ')` for `κ' ∈ (4, 8]`; Gwynne–Holden–Miller–Sun, arXiv:1510.04687,
Theorem 1.1: the same correlation for `κ' > 8`); its fibres are the classes of the relation generated by the direct
identifications (DMS, Lemmas 8.9 and 8.15, pp. 143-144, 148). At a fixed time `u` each
coordinate of the pair goes strictly below its value at `u` arbitrarily close to `u` on both sides, so `u` is the only
time of `g(u)`, and `g(u)` is an interior point of `g([a, b])` (Berestycki–Gwynne, arXiv:2003.10320, p. 62: "such a
component exists a.s. since a.s. `η(u) ∉ ∂η([a, b])`"). The frontier of the component is a Jordan curve
(Berestycki–Gwynne, proof of Lemma 7.21, p. 64: "In particular, `∂U` is a Jordan curve"): for `κ' ≥ 8`, `g([a, b])`
has the topology of a closed disk (Berestycki–Gwynne, Section 2.2.2, p. 16); for `4 < κ' < 8` the interiors of the
past and future traces at a fixed time are chains of Jordan domains (Gwynne–Sung, Ann. Probab., p. 672, with the
stationarity (1.9) of DMS), and a component of the intersection of two Jordan domains is a Jordan domain
(Kerékjártó, in the form of Bonino, Proc. Amer. Math. Soc., Section 3, before Proposition 3.1). -/
def WindowFrontierJordanGenuineCurve : Prop :=
  ∀ γ : ℝ, 0 < γ → γ < 2 → ∀ μ : Measure Path2, IsCorrelatedBM2 γ μ →
    ∀ a u b : ℝ, a < u → u < b →
    ∀ᵐ p ∂μ, ∃ g : ℝ → ℂ, Continuous g ∧ Function.Surjective g ∧
      Tendsto g (cocompact ℝ) (cocompact ℂ) ∧
      (∀ s t, (directRel (fun v => (p v).1) s t ∨ directRel (fun v => (p v).2) s t) → g s = g t) ∧
      (∀ s t, g s = g t → Relation.EqvGen
        (fun x y => directRel (fun v => (p v).1) x y ∨ directRel (fun v => (p v).2) x y) s t) ∧
      g u ∈ interior (g '' Icc a b) ∧
      LQGMetric.JordanMap.IsJordanCurve
        (frontier (connectedComponentIn (interior (g '' Icc a b)) (g u)))

end MatedCRT
